course: dictionary
lesson: 14704
title: Prise: force it open
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Prise means to force something open or apart, often by pushing a tool into a gap and levering it. «Sam prised the paint can open with a flat screwdriver.» The clues are the tool and the gap: you need force to move a lid that is stuck.
explain: Prise can also mean to get something out of someone with effort. «The detective prised the truth out of the nervous thief.» The thief did not want to talk, so the detective had to work hard to get the answer.
explain: Prise often comes with small words that show direction: prise open, prise off, prise out of, prise apart. «The twins prised the two halves of the orange apart.» Those little words tell you where the force goes.
explain: Try the swap test. Put «force open» into the sentence instead of prise. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Prise means to force something open or apart, often using a tool as a lever.
fact: Prise can also mean to get a secret or information out of someone with effort.
fact: In the US the same word is spelled pry.
fact: Common phrases are prise open, prise off, prise out of and prise apart.
fact: Prise is also a rare noun meaning leverage, the power a lever gives you.
exercise: Sam used a coin to prise open the lid of the tin. What does prise mean? | *Force open with a tool | Shake hard | Throw away | Paint with a brush
exercise: The detective tried to prise the secret out of the suspect. What does prise mean here? | *Get something out with effort | Sing a song loudly | Drop something quickly | Hide something safely
exercise: Which word means the same as prise, to force open? | *Pry | Wrap | Melt | Whisper
test: What does prise mean in «The gardener used a crowbar to prise the rusty gate off its hinges»? | Watered | Admired | *Forced off with a tool | Painted
test: Why did Nina prise the story out of her brother? | She read the story aloud | She forgot the story completely | *She forced him, with effort, to tell her what happened | She gave him a present
exam: What clue shows that prise means force a lid open? | *The lid was stuck, so Tom had to prise it off the jar | Tom sat down to rest | The jar was empty | The lid was pretty
